course: arts
lesson: 16
title: Famous artists, simply (Van Gogh, Kahlo, Picasso, Hokusai, Bilokur, Prymachenko, Qi Baishi...)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Famous artists were real people with real lives, and knowing their story often explains their pictures: what they could see, what hurt them, what they loved and what they were allowed to do.
explain: Picture Frida Kahlo, badly hurt in a bus crash as a teenager, lying in bed for months with a mirror above her: she painted what she saw, herself, and her self-portraits show pain, Mexican culture and strong feelings.
explain: Many artists mastered the rules before breaking them: Picasso could draw perfectly at 15 and later chose to break faces into shapes; Qi Baishi made a shrimp come alive with a few ink strokes after years of practice.
explain: Fame is not fair or quick: Van Gogh sold very few paintings while alive, and for centuries women were often kept out of art schools, so historians are still finding forgotten artists; who liked whose art is often argued, but the dates and facts can be checked.
explain: People talk about it in their own slang: "bummer" is older US slang for a disappointment, "sus" means suspicious or odd, and a "glow-up" is a big change for the better.
fact: Van Gogh sold very few paintings and was supported by his brother Theo; he died in 1890 at 37, and his fame grew after his death, helped by Theo's wife Jo.
fact: Frida Kahlo, a Mexican painter, was badly hurt in a bus crash as a teenager and painted with a mirror above her bed; a self-portrait is a picture an artist makes of themselves.
fact: Picasso was trained from a young child and could draw very realistically as a teenager; later he developed Cubism with Georges Braque, showing things from several sides at once.
fact: Maria Prymachenko was a Ukrainian folk artist from the village of Bolotnia who painted bright, imaginary animals.
fact: In 2022 a museum near Kyiv holding Prymachenko's work was burned during the Russian invasion, and local people were reported to have saved many of her paintings.
fact: Qi Baishi was a famous 20th-century Chinese painter of simple ink paintings of shrimps, crabs, birds and flowers; he was a carpenter before becoming a painter, he became famous in his own lifetime, and today his paintings sell for millions.
fact: Leonardo da Vinci, an Italian of the Renaissance, filled notebooks with drawings of machines, animals, water and the human body; most of his machines were never built.
fact: The Renaissance was a period of new learning and art in Europe from around 1400 to 1600.
fact: Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling between 1508 and 1512 standing on a scaffold and bending his head back, not lying down; he wrote a funny poem about his aching neck.
fact: Georgia O'Keeffe, an American painter, painted flowers huge so people would really look at them, and later painted animal skulls and hills in the New Mexico desert, where she lived, because she found them beautiful.
fact: Jean-Michel Basquiat started as a graffiti writer in New York, painted powerful pictures with words, crowns and faces in the 1980s, became famous young and died at 27.
fact: For centuries women were often kept out of art academies; some, like Artemisia Gentileschi in 1600s Italy, still became famous, and many others were forgotten.
fact: Hokusai, the Japanese artist of The Great Wave, made thousands of prints and drawings over a long life; Kateryna Bilokur, a self-taught Ukrainian painter, painted incredibly detailed flowers.
exercise: Van Gogh sold almost nothing while alive. What happened later? | His fame grew after his death, helped by Theo's wife Jo | He was famous and rich all his life, selling every painting he made | His paintings were all lost for ever | He stopped painting after one try and became a farmer instead
exercise: Who supported Van Gogh while he painted? | His brother Theo | The king of the Netherlands | His friend Picasso | A big art company in Paris
exercise: Sofia's brother says Kahlo's self-portraits are "self-obsessed". What explains them? | After a bus crash she was in bed with a mirror above her | She thought she was the most beautiful woman alive in Mexico | She was not allowed to paint anything else | She never painted anyone but herself, by order of the Mexican law
exercise: Harry says Picasso "couldn't draw". What is true? | He drew realistically as a teen, then chose Cubism | He never learned to draw anything real, even as a grown man | He only drew faces with two eyes on one side | His teacher drew all his early work for him and signed it
exercise: Who developed Cubism with Picasso? | Georges Braque | Vincent van Gogh | Frida Kahlo | Qi Baishi
exercise: Olena's friend calls Prymachenko's beasts "Pokémon but Ukrainian". Who was she? | A Ukrainian village folk artist who painted imaginary animals | A cartoonist from Japan who designed the very first real Pokémon | A city painter of portraits of kings | A photographer who took pictures of real farm animals in the village
exercise: What happened to a museum with Prymachenko's work in 2022? | It was burned during the Russian invasion, and locals reportedly saved many works | It sold all her paintings at a big auction in London to raise money for repairs | It moved to Paris to be safe | Nothing happened to it at all during the war, as it was far from any fighting
exercise: Ming thinks Qi Baishi's shrimps are "sus" (odd) for millions. What is true? | Making a shrimp alive in a few strokes takes years of skill | Anyone could paint those shrimps in a minute with no practice | The shrimps were traced from a photo | Simple pictures are always the easiest ones to paint, so they should be cheap
exercise: What was Qi Baishi before he was a painter? | A carpenter | A banker | A pilot | A doctor
exercise: Ava learns Leonardo drew flying machines. What does that show? | He used drawing to understand how things work | Art and science are complete opposites that never meet | All his machines flew perfectly | He only drew pretty faces and never looked at machines
exercise: Joe's dad says Michelangelo lay on his back. What is true? | He stood on a scaffold and bent his head back | He lay flat on his back on a bed for four whole years | He painted the ceiling on the floor first | He used a very long brush, standing on the chapel floor
exercise: When did Michelangelo paint the Sistine Chapel ceiling? | Between 1508 and 1512 | In the 1980s | In 1890 | In 2022
exercise: Grace thinks O'Keeffe's desert bones are creepy. Why did she paint them? | She found them beautiful in the desert where she lived | She was told by a gallery to paint only scary things | She only had bones to paint | Bones were the fashion in every gallery in America at the time
exercise: Kwame says Basquiat's story is a glow-up. What is true? | He went from graffiti in New York to famous painter | He only ever painted neat flowers in a quiet studio | He went to art school for twenty years | He never showed his work in a gallery, only on walls
exercise: Lily asks why most famous old painters are men. What is the answer? | Women were often kept out of art schools or forgotten | Women never made any art at all in the past | Men are born better at painting | Only men were allowed to buy paint in the shops
exercise: Who was Artemisia Gentileschi? | A famous woman painter in 1600s Italy | A Mexican painter hurt in a bus crash | A Chinese ink painter of shrimps and crabs | A Ukrainian folk artist
test: Your mate goes, "if your art's good, people notice straight away." Which artist proves him wrong? | Van Gogh, who sold very few paintings in his life | Picasso, who was famous young | Basquiat, who became famous young in New York | Qi Baishi, who became famous while he lived
test: Harry's teacher shows Picasso's drawing at 15. What lesson does that teach? | Breaking the rules works best once you know them | Picasso was always bad at drawing, even at fifteen | Only teens can draw realistically | Cubism was a mistake he couldn't fix, however hard he tried
test: Teacher's dad says "Bummer" about Van Gogh being broke. What does he mean? | That's a disappointment | That's brilliant news for us | That's a big lie | That's very funny
test: A cartoon shows Michelangelo snoozing on his back on a bed while painting. What should it show? | Him standing on a scaffold, head bent back | Him painting from a hot-air balloon | Him painting on the floor | Him painting the ceiling with his feet
test: Ming's grandpa says simple is the hardest. What would happen if Ming tried to paint a live-looking shrimp in five strokes? | It would most likely be much harder than it looks | It would be perfect the very first time | It would be easy, as it's only shrimps | The ink would refuse to stick to the paper at all
test: Sofia's brother calls Kahlo vain. What would be a fairer first step? | Learn about her life before judging her work | Ignore her life and judge only the faces | Say all self-portraits are vain, whoever paints them | Only judge her paintings of flowers
test: Your uncle reckons "women didn't paint in the old days". What is true? | Many did but were blocked or forgotten | He's right, no women painted before 1900 | Women were only allowed to paint shrimps | Women painted, but only men's portraits
test: Olena says Prymachenko's animals are imaginary. Is that a weakness? | No, imagination can matter as much as copying life | Yes, real art must always copy real animals | Yes, village artists don't count | No, because they are real animals from Bolotnia, her village
test: Ava says Leonardo "was an artist AND a scientist, that's mad". Which is true? | He used drawing to study machines, water and bodies | He never studied anything but paint and brushes | His machines were all built and sold in shops in Italy | He was only a scientist, not an artist at all
test: Kwame's cousin says only neat art becomes famous. Whose story shows otherwise? | Basquiat, whose raw style became highly valued | O'Keeffe, who only ever painted tiny neat dots | Michelangelo, who never painted a thing | Leonardo, who only wrote poems and songs, never paintings
test: What would have happened to Prymachenko's paintings in the museum fire if locals hadn't acted, as was reported? | Many more of them would likely have been lost | They would have been sold for millions | They would have been safe in the fire anyway | They would have moved to China
exam: Identify the principal circumstance which gave rise to the numerous self-portraits of Frida Kahlo. | Prolonged confinement to bed after a road accident, with a mirror placed above her | A conviction that she possessed unrivalled beauty | A legal prohibition upon the depiction of other subjects by women painters in Mexico | A commission from the government of Mexico
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the painting of the Sistine Chapel ceiling by Michelangelo. | It was executed between 1508 and 1512 in a standing posture upon a scaffold | It was executed while the artist lay supine upon a couch for its entire duration | It was executed upon the floor and subsequently raised | It was executed in the twentieth century
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the commercial fortunes of Vincent van Gogh during his lifetime? | He sold very few paintings and was supported by his brother | He enjoyed great wealth from the sale of his works throughout his life | He sold every work upon its completion | He declined to sell any work whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the reason most widely advanced for the predominance of men among celebrated painters of former centuries. | The frequent exclusion of women from academies of art and their subsequent neglect | The incapacity of women to employ paint | The absence of any woman who wished to paint in the centuries before photography | The prohibition of the sale of paint to women
